"type":99,"typeText":"∀ {α : Type u_1} {β : Type u_2} {R : α → β → Prop}\n  {motive : (a : List.{u_1} α) → (a_1 : List.{u_2} β) → List.Forall₂.{u_1, u_2} R a a_1 → Prop},\n  motive List.nil.{u_1} List.nil.{u_2} ⋯ →\n    (∀ {a : α} {b : β} {l₁ : List.{u_1} α} {l₂ : List.{u_2} β} (a_1 : R a b) (a_2 : List.Forall₂.{u_1, u_2} R l₁ l₂),\n        motive l₁ l₂ a_2 → motive (List.cons.{u_1} a l₁) (List.cons.{u_2} b l₂) ⋯) →\n      ∀ {a : List.{u_1} α} {a_1 : List.{u_2} β} (t : List.Forall₂.{u_1, u_2} R a a_1), motive a a_1 t","unsafe":false,"value":null,"source":{"code":"/--\n`Forall₂ R l₁ l₂` means that `l₁` and `l₂` have the same length,\nand whenever `a` is the nth element of `l₁`, and `b` is the nth element of `l₂`,\nthen `R a b` is satisfied.\n-/\ninductive Forall₂ (R : α → β → Prop) : List α → List β → Prop\n  /-- Two nil lists are `Forall₂`-related -/\n  | nil : Forall₂ R [] []\n  /-- Two cons lists are related by `Forall₂ R`\n  if the heads are related by `R` and the tails are related by `Forall₂ R` -/\n  | cons {a b l₁ l₂} : R a b → Forall₂ R l₁ l₂ → Forall₂ R (a :: l₁) (b :: l₂)","start":374,"end":384},"axioms":[]}